WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.938
Hi! Ice cream melts in the sun, and water freezes in the freezer. Why? Today let's learn how water changes state.

2
00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:15.282
Water changes state when it gains heat or loses heat.

3
00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:20.610
When ice gains heat, it melts into water. This is called melting.

4
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.082
When water loses heat, it turns into ice. This is called freezing.

5
00:00:26.232 --> 00:00:39.954
When water gains heat, it turns into water vapour. At 100 degrees it bubbles all through. That is boiling. When it slowly turns into vapour from the surface at any temperature, that is evaporation.

6
00:00:40.104 --> 00:00:48.330
When water vapour loses heat, it turns back into water droplets. This is called condensation.

7
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:59.538
The melting point of pure water is 0 degrees Celsius. Ice starts to melt at 0 degrees. The freezing point of water is also 0 degrees Celsius.

8
00:00:59.688 --> 00:01:07.242
The boiling point of pure water is 100 degrees Celsius. At 100 degrees, water boils.

9
00:01:07.392 --> 00:01:17.874
But evaporation is different. It happens at any temperature. A puddle dries up slowly without ever reaching 100 degrees.

10
00:01:18.024 --> 00:01:22.386
Let's heat some ice, measure its temperature, and draw this graph.

11
00:01:22.536 --> 00:01:29.682
While the ice is melting, it keeps gaining heat, but the temperature stays at 0 degrees until all the ice has melted.

12
00:01:29.832 --> 00:01:35.826
It's the same while the water is boiling. The temperature stays at 100 degrees.

13
00:01:35.976 --> 00:01:44.082
So gaining heat does not always make the temperature rise. The heat may be changing the state instead.

14
00:01:44.232 --> 00:01:53.850
Droplets form on the outside of a glass of ice water. Water vapour in the air touches the cold glass, loses heat and condenses into droplets.

15
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.682
Wet clothes dry because the water evaporates into water vapour and goes into the air.

16
00:01:59.832 --> 00:02:05.754
An ice cube on a table gains heat from the surrounding air and melts.

17
00:02:05.904 --> 00:02:13.002
Watch out! Some pupils say the droplets on a cold glass leaked out from inside. That's wrong!

18
00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:22.146
The glass doesn't leak. The droplets come from water vapour in the air. It touches the cold glass, loses heat and condenses.

19
00:02:22.296 --> 00:02:28.986
Remember: when water vapour loses heat, it condenses into droplets.

20
00:02:29.136 --> 00:02:38.442
Your turn! While ice is melting, what happens to its temperature? Pause and think.

21
00:02:38.592 --> 00:02:45.906
It stays at 0 degrees Celsius until all the ice has melted. Did you get it right?

22
00:02:46.056 --> 00:02:56.034
Another one! When water turns into ice, does it gain heat or lose heat? Pause and think.

23
00:02:56.184 --> 00:03:02.682
It loses heat. This is called freezing. Did you get it right?

24
00:03:02.832 --> 00:03:11.562
Last one! How is evaporation different from boiling? Pause and think.

25
00:03:11.712 --> 00:03:22.170
Evaporation happens at any temperature, only at the surface. Boiling happens at 100 degrees. Did you get it right?

26
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:38.754
Remember: gaining heat makes water melt, evaporate or boil. Losing heat makes it freeze or condense. Pure water melts and freezes at 0 degrees, and boils at 100 degrees. While it changes state, the temperature stays the same.

27
00:03:38.904 --> 00:03:43.146
Go and check your freezer for frost!

